MAP
The map() function is a built-in Python function that applies a given function to every element in an iterable (such as a list or tuple) and returns the transformed results as an iterator.

In simple words, map() is used to perform an operation on every element of a list.

Syntax:
map(function, iterable)

REDUCE
The reduce() function applies a function cumulatively to the elements of an iterable and reduces them into a single value.

In simple words, reduce() is used to combine all elements of a list into one final result.

It is available in the functools module.

Syntax:
from functools import reduce
reduce(function, iterable)


Example:
from functools import reduce

lis1= [4,3,2,5,6,7,8,9]

evens = list(filter(lambda n : n%2 ==0 , lis1))
doubles = list(map(lambda n : n *2, evens))
Total = reduce(lambda a,b: a+b,doubles)

print("evens : ", evens)
print("doubles : ", doubles)
print("Total :", Total)
